5"}], "model": {"prior_margin": 3.68, "prior_sd": 5.11, "poll_margin": null, "poll_sd": null, "poll_weight": 0.0, "fundamentals": {"margin": 3.5, "lean": -3.0, "environment": 8.4, "incumbency": -2.0, "money": 0.1}, "rating_margin": 3.8, "sigma_race": 6.5, "sigma_total": 7.2, "baseline_margin": 3.68, "analyst_adjustment": -0.18, "key_factors": ["Expert ratings lean toward a Democratic flip, with several calling it Lean D and others Tossup", "Prediction markets price Cooke around 73% to win", "Van Orden carries incumbency in an R+3 district amid a favorable Democratic environment", "Cooke has out-raised Van Orden, though he holds a large cash advantage"], "rationale": "The fundamentals point to a narrow Democratic edge: the district's Republican lean is offset by a strong national environment for Democrats, and most expert raters now call the race Lean D or Tossup, with markets pricing Cooke near 73%. Polling is sparse and dated, all Democratic-sponsored, so the forecast stays close to the fundamentals-based estimate of a few points for Cooke.", "watch": "Fresh independent polls and the late-October debates could shift the picture, especially if the AI deepfake controversy or Van Orden's cash advantage moves voters.", "confidence": "medium", "notes": ["No usable general-election polls; forecast rests on fundamentals and expert ratings.", "Incumbent Derrick Van Orden (R) is running."]}, "overview": "Western Wisconsin's third district is one of the most competitive House races of 2026. Republican incumbent Derrick Van Orden, first elected in 2022 in an R+3 seat, faces Democrat Rebecca Cooke, who has out-raised him but trails in cash on hand. The national political environment favors Democrats this cycle, and most expert raters now place the race in the Lean D to Tossup range, with several forecasting a flip.\n\nThe campaign has grown contentious in its final weeks. Van Orden drew criticism for publishing AI-generated videos of Cooke, prompting a cease-and-desist letter,